 "answer": "Yes, the haze in KL is bad right now. The most recent reports I found are from 16 October 2026.\n\n- **Kuala Lumpur:** Malay Mail reported that Batu Muda and Cheras both had an API of 163 as of 5.30pm. Putrajaya was at 170.\n- **Category:** Malaysia classes 101–200 as \"unhealthy\". Readings of 201–300 are \"very unhealthy\". KL is in the unhealthy band, not the worst one.\n- **Nearby areas:** Several Klang Valley stations were in the same range, including Klang (176), Petaling Jaya (173) and Shah Alam (168). Johan Setia was at 184. The area around KLIA/Sepang was reported at about 197.\n- **Nationwide:** About 29–30 areas were unhealthy. Pasir Gudang and Larkin in Johor reached very unhealthy levels, at 202–207.\n- **Cause:** The haze is linked to peatland fires in Indonesia. Schools were reportedly closed earlier this month because of it.\n\n**What to do**\n- Limit prolonged outdoor activity and exercise outdoors.\n- Wear a well-fitting N95 mask if you need to go out.\n- Keep windows closed and use an air purifier or air-conditioning if you have one.\n- Children, older people, and anyone with asthma, heart or lung conditions should be especially careful.\n\nReadings change hourly. For the current figure, check the Department of Environment's APIMS or a tracker such as semakapi.my.",
